# cloud-armor-rules.yaml.example - Cloud Armor security policy
#
# NOT YET DOGFOODED against the live GCP platform.
# Claims verified against GCP documentation as of 2026-06-09.
# See extras/gcp/README.md §External-claim verification status.
#
# Apply to the load balancer backend service in front of Cloud Run + IAP.
# This policy runs BEFORE IAP, so unauthenticated flood traffic is blocked
# at the edge before reaching the IAP authentication layer.
#
# Apply:
#   gcloud compute backend-services update <your-agent-name>-backend \
#     --global \
#     --security-policy <policy-name>
#
# The rules below are a starting point. Tune thresholds for your traffic
# profile; agent.call() can take 5-120s so per-IP burst allowances should
# account for legitimate concurrent users, not just bots.
#
# TIER NOTE:
#   Cloud Armor has exactly two tiers: Standard and Cloud Armor Enterprise.
#   There is no "free" or "base" tier. Every project with an external load
#   balancer is automatically enrolled in Cloud Armor Standard (pay-as-you-go),
#   and all rules below run on Standard:
#     Rule 500  (body-size content-length match) - Standard.
#     Rule 900  (enforceOnKey: HTTP_HEADER rate limit) - Standard; no Enterprise
#               subscription required.
#     Rule 1000 (IP-keyed rate limit) - Standard.
#     Rule 2000 (preconfigured WAF XSS) - Standard. (Managed Protection Plus /
#               Adaptive Protection is the separate Enterprise paid feature and
#               is NOT required here.)
#   Inspect your security policy tier with:
#     gcloud compute security-policies describe <policy-name> --format=value(type)
# (Verified: Standard vs Enterprise tiers at
#  https://cloud.google.com/armor/docs/armor-enterprise-overview)
#
# AGGREGATE SPEND CONTROL NOTE (CWE-770):
#   Cloud Armor rate limits cap per-IP (Rule 1000, any topology) and per-identity
#   (Rule 900, CLASSIC ALB only - see the LOAD-BALANCER ORDERING note below)
#   REQUEST rates, but neither bounds aggregate agent.call() spend across all
#   users. A billing budget alert is the only project-level ceiling on total LLM
#   spend. See extras/gcp/README.md §"Cost controls and billing budget" for setup.
#
# LOAD-BALANCER ORDERING - READ BEFORE RELYING ON RULE 900:
#   The per-identity rule (Rule 900) keys on X-Goog-Authenticated-User-Email,
#   which IAP injects only AFTER it authenticates a request. Whether that header
#   is present when Cloud Armor evaluates depends ENTIRELY on the load-balancer
#   type:
#     - Global external ALB (EXTERNAL_MANAGED, the scheme iap-setup.md
#       prescribes): Cloud Armor evaluates FIRST, then IAP. The IAP identity
#       header is therefore NOT present at Cloud Armor evaluation time, so Rule
#       900 sees no identity key and collapses ALL traffic into one empty-key
#       bucket. On this topology Rule 900 is NOT a working per-identity control.
#     - Classic ALB (EXTERNAL): IAP evaluates FIRST, then Cloud Armor. The
#       identity header IS present, so Rule 900 keys per-identity as intended.
#   (Verified: "For a backend service of a global external Application Load
#    Balancer, Cloud Armor evaluation happens first ... If Cloud Armor allows a
#    request, IAP then evaluates the request" and the inverse for the classic
#    ALB, at https://cloud.google.com/armor/docs/integrating-cloud-armor)
#   CONSEQUENCE: On the blueprint's default global external ALB, do NOT treat
#   Rule 900 as a per-identity spend control - it is a no-op there. Rely on the
#   billing budget (the only aggregate ceiling) + Rule 1000 (per-IP). If you
#   specifically want per-identity limiting, deploy on the classic ALB
#   (--load-balancing-scheme=EXTERNAL) so IAP runs first.

rules:
  # Rule 500: deny requests with large bodies (OOM guard, issue #401).
  # A large request body sent to /call can cause the serve layer to read it
  # into memory before the agent starts, contributing to OOM kills.
  #
  # Cloud Armor Standard tier supports request size limits via the
  # evaluatePreconfiguredExpr('methodenforcement-v0') or content-length header
  # matching. The expression below uses the request.headers content-length check.
  #
  # The serve layer's own body-size limit (Starlette max_body_size, if
  # configured) is the primary gate; this Cloud Armor rule is defense-in-depth.
  # agent.call() work_item payloads are plain text, not file uploads - 32KB is
  # a generous ceiling for normal use.
  #
  # Cloud Armor CEL supports int() casting on header values for numeric
  # comparison. The rules-language reference uses
  # `int(request.headers["content-length"]) == 0` as a canonical example, so an
  # exact byte threshold (32768 = 32KB) is expressible directly - no digit-count
  # regex approximation needed.
  # (Verified: int() on content-length header at
  #  https://cloud.google.com/armor/docs/rules-language-reference)
  - priority: 500
    description: "Deny requests exceeding 32KB body (OOM guard, issue #401)"
    match:
      expr:
        expression: >
          has(request.headers['content-length']) &&
          int(request.headers['content-length']) > 32768
    action: deny(403)
    # NOTE: Cloud Armor cannot return a literal 413 (Payload Too Large). For a
    # standard (non-rate-limit) MATCH-deny rule like this one, the deny action
    # supports only deny-403, deny-404, and deny-502
    # (gcloud: --action=[ allow | deny-403 | deny-404 | deny-502 ]). 403 is used
    # here as the closest valid code for a rejected oversized request.
    # (The rate-limit exceedAction is a SEPARATE action set: Rule 900 below uses
    # deny(429), which GCP recommends for rate-limited responses - see
    # https://cloud.google.com/armor/docs/rate-limiting-overview. So 429 is valid
    # for rate-limit rules, just not for this standard match-deny rule.)
    # A true 413 would originate at a serve-layer body-size gate, which does NOT
    # exist today (#401 is open). Until #401 ships, this Cloud Armor rule is the
    # only body-size control; once it ships, the serve-layer gate becomes the
    # authoritative one.
    # (Verified: standard match-deny status codes at
    #  https://cloud.google.com/armor/docs/configure-security-policies ;
    #  rate-limit deny-429 at
    #  https://cloud.google.com/armor/docs/rate-limiting-overview)
    #
    # int(content-length) > 32768 denies bodies larger than 32KB exactly.
    # Tune the byte threshold for your payload ceiling.
    #
    # FAILS OPEN for two cases - this rule is defense-in-depth, NOT a complete
    # OOM defense against a hostile caller:
    #   1. Requests with NO Content-Length header (e.g. Transfer-Encoding:
    #      chunked) are not matched by has(content-length) and pass this rule.
    #   2. The declared Content-Length can lie; only the actual streamed body
    #      size is authoritative.
    # A serve-layer max_body_size gate does NOT exist today (#401 is open), so
    # this Cloud Armor rule is currently the only body-size control. Once #401
    # ships, the serve-layer gate becomes the authoritative one. Do NOT treat
    # this rule as closing the #401 OOM surface (it fails open on the two cases
    # above).

  # Rule 900: per-identity rate limit - WORKS ONLY ON THE CLASSIC ALB.
  #
  # DECISIVE TOPOLOGY FACT (read before relying on this rule):
  #   IAP injects X-Goog-Authenticated-User-Email only AFTER it authenticates.
  #   On the GLOBAL EXTERNAL ALB (EXTERNAL_MANAGED) that iap-setup.md prescribes,
  #   Cloud Armor evaluates BEFORE IAP, so this header is ABSENT at Rule 900's
  #   evaluation time on the LEGITIMATE authenticated path. Every authenticated
  #   request therefore collapses into ONE shared empty-key bucket and Rule 900
  #   delivers NO per-identity isolation on that topology - it is a no-op there.
  #   (Verified: "For a backend service of a global external Application Load
  #    Balancer, Cloud Armor evaluation happens first ... If Cloud Armor allows a
  #    request, IAP then evaluates the request"; the classic ALB evaluates IAP
  #    first, at https://cloud.google.com/armor/docs/integrating-cloud-armor)
  #
  #   To get real per-identity limiting you must deploy on the CLASSIC ALB
  #   (--load-balancing-scheme=EXTERNAL), where IAP runs first and the identity
  #   header is present when Cloud Armor evaluates. On the default global external
  #   ALB, do NOT count Rule 900 as a spend control: lean on the billing budget
  #   (the only aggregate ceiling - README §"Cost controls and billing budget")
  #   plus the IP-keyed Rule 1000.
  #
  # The rule below is scoped with has(...) so it applies ONLY to requests that
  # actually carry the identity header (i.e. the classic-ALB authenticated path).
  # This prevents header-less traffic (unauthenticated requests, and the
  # same-project OIDC/internal Cloud-Scheduler-direct-to-run.app path documented
  # in iap-setup.md / cloudscheduler-jobs.yaml.example) from collapsing into a
  # shared empty-key bucket and being collaterally banned by rate_based_ban for
  # banDurationSec. Header-less traffic is left to Rule 1000's per-IP cap.
  #
  # REMAINING LIMITATIONS (even on the classic ALB where Rule 900 functions):
  #   (1) The X-Goog-Authenticated-User-Email header is UNSIGNED and forgeable if
  #       IAP is bypassed. Its trustworthiness comes from IAP being enforced in
  #       front (the ingress lock + Rule 1000 IP cap partially backstop a bypass),
  #       not from this rule. An attacker who reaches the backend around IAP can
  #       rotate forged identities to get a fresh rate bucket each time.
  #       (Verified: header is unsigned / IAP-enforcement-dependent at
  #        https://cloud.google.com/iap/docs/signed-headers-howto)
  #   (2) This rule caps per-identity REQUEST rate, not per-identity LLM spend. A
  #       single request can trigger an agent.call() with unbounded token cost.
  #   (3) For an aggregate project-level spend ceiling, use a GCP billing budget
  #       alert (see README §"Cost controls and billing budget") - it is the ONLY
  #       control that bounds total LLM spend regardless of number of identities
  #       or IPs, and the ONLY control covering the header-less OIDC/internal path.
  #   (4) IP-keyed Rule 1000 does NOT bound aggregate spend either - a principal
  #       cycling IPs bypasses IP-level caps while this identity-keyed cap still
  #       applies (when the header is present).
  #
  # enforceOnKey: HTTP_HEADER rate limiting runs on Cloud Armor Standard, the
  # default tier for any project with an external load balancer; no Enterprise
  # subscription is required.
  # Verified: enforceOnKey: HTTP_HEADER at
  # https://cloud.google.com/armor/docs/rate-limiting-overview
  - priority: 900
    description: "Rate limit per authenticated identity (classic ALB only; no-op on global external ALB)"
    match:
      # Apply only to requests carrying the IAP identity header. On the global
      # external ALB this match is never true (header absent pre-IAP), so the
      # rule no-ops cleanly instead of banning the shared empty-key bucket.
      expr:
        expression: "has(request.headers['x-goog-authenticated-user-email'])"
    action: rate_based_ban
    rateLimitOptions:
      conformAction: allow
      exceedAction: deny(429)
      enforceOnKey: HTTP_HEADER
      enforceOnKeyName: X-Goog-Authenticated-User-Email
      rateLimitThreshold:
        count: 60
        intervalSec: 60
      banDurationSec: 300

  # Rule 1000: per-IP rate limit - blocks burst traffic before IAP.
  # Tune count + interval for your expected interactive usage.
  #
  # NOTE: IP-keyed limiting does NOT bound aggregate agent.call() spend.
  # An authenticated principal cycling IPs can exceed this per-IP cap while
  # staying under the identity-keyed cap (Rule 900) if their requests come
  # from different IPs. Use the billing budget (README §Cost controls) as
  # the aggregate project-level ceiling.
  - priority: 1000
    description: "Rate limit: 20 requests per minute per IP"
    match:
      versionedExpr: SRC_IPS_V1
      config:
        srcIpRanges:
          - "*"
    action: rate_based_ban
    rateLimitOptions:
      conformAction: allow
      exceedAction: deny(429)
      enforceOnKey: IP
      rateLimitThreshold:
        count: 20
        intervalSec: 60
      banDurationSec: 300

  # Rule 2000: block XSS-pattern request payloads via a preconfigured WAF
  # signature. Preconfigured WAF rules run on the Standard Cloud Armor tier.
  #
  # WAF rule set versions are versioned and updated by GCP. Verify the current
  # stable version identifier before deploying:
  #   https://cloud.google.com/armor/docs/rule-tuning
  # At time of writing the current stable XSS preconfigured rule set is
  # xss-v422-stable (OWASP CRS 4.22). The older xss-v33-stable (CRS 3.x) still
  # functions but is the legacy set. Confirm the latest stable version at the
  # rules-language reference before deploying.
  - priority: 2000
    description: "Block XSS-pattern request payloads (preconfigured WAF)"
    match:
      expr:
        expression: "evaluatePreconfiguredWaf('xss-v422-stable')"
    action: deny(403)

  # Rule 3000: allow all other traffic (passes to IAP for authentication).
  - priority: 3000
    description: "Default allow - IAP handles authentication"
    match:
      versionedExpr: SRC_IPS_V1
      config:
        srcIpRanges:
          - "*"
    action: allow
